# Linear Response Demonstrator

Specification: `scientific-onnx-model-package` `0.1-draft`<br>
Package identifier: `org.fortonnx.examples.linear-response`<br>
Package version: `example-1`<br>
Release status: `research`<br>
Release date: `2026-08-22`

Linear Response Demonstrator is a deterministic ONNX model that maps a
temperature anomaly and radiative forcing to an illustrative temperature
response. It demonstrates a complete scientific model package. Its coefficients
are not derived from observations, experiments, or simulations and are not
scientifically validated.

Responsible organization: FortONNX contributors<br>
Contact: FortONNX project maintainers<br>
License: BSD-2-Clause for the complete example package<br>
Preferred citation: see `CITATION.txt`

## Intended use

Use this package to study ONNX package discovery, exact tensor binding, unit and
domain metadata, artifact integrity, and known-answer verification. The
supported operating mode is one independent invocation. Domain expertise is
not required beyond understanding that the formula is illustrative rather than
physical evidence.

| Condition | Supported value | Evidence |
|---|---|---|
| Task | Package integration and verification | `known-answer-evidence` |
| Batch size | 1 or 3 | `dynamic-batch`, `nominal` |
| Temperature anomaly | -10 K to 10 K | Interface and verification checks |
| Radiative forcing | -500 W m^-2 to 500 W m^-2 | Interface and verification checks |
| Operating mode | Single-pass | `physical-response` process graph |
| Runtime | ONNX ReferenceEvaluator from ONNX @@ONNX_VERSION@@ | `verification/reference-report.json` |

## Unsupported uses

- scientific prediction, calibration, inference, or publication of physical
  results;
- climate, weather, engineering, safety, regulatory, or policy decisions;
- autoregressive, iterative, or coupled simulation;
- claims outside the exact known-answer and interface behavior documented here;
- interpretation of the coefficients as estimated physical parameters.

No human oversight procedure can make this demonstrator suitable for a
scientific decision. Replace it with a validated model and evidence package for
such use.

## Package contents

Machine-readable package description: `package-description.json`<br>
Description format: UTF-8 JSON using mapping version 1<br>
Abstract-model mapping: `metadata/abstract-model-mapping.md`<br>
Mapping identifier: `org.fortonnx.examples.direct-json` version 1<br>
Mapping digest: `sha256:@@MAPPING_SHA256@@`

The expected package-description digest is stored outside the description's
digest closure in `package-description.sha256`. No publisher signature or
authenticated release record is supplied; the detached digest detects mismatch
only when obtained through a separately trusted channel.

The detached digest-record layout is UTF-8 text version 1: algorithm name,
one ASCII space, lowercase hexadecimal digest, one ASCII space, relative
filename, and newline.

| Artifact | Path | Role | Required | Format and version | Reader | SHA-256 |
|---|---|---|---:|---|---|---|
| Package description | `package-description.json` | Package index | yes | UTF-8 JSON, direct mapping 1 | JSON reader and declared mapping | detached record |
| Model Card | `README.md` | Interpretation and documentation | yes | UTF-8 Markdown | Text reader | indexed digest |
| Primary graph | `models/linear-response.onnx` | Inference | yes | ONNX IR 9, opset 18 | Compatible ONNX runtime | `@@MODEL_SHA256@@` |
| Abstract mapping | `metadata/abstract-model-mapping.md` | Interpretation | yes | UTF-8 Markdown | Text reader | `@@MAPPING_SHA256@@` |
| Known-answer data | `verification/nominal.csv` | Verification | yes | UTF-8 CSV, example layout 1 | CSV reader using declared header | `@@VERIFICATION_CSV_SHA256@@` |
| Reference report | `verification/reference-report.json` | Verification evidence | yes | UTF-8 JSON, report 1 | JSON reader | `@@REFERENCE_REPORT_SHA256@@` |
| Reference example | `examples/reference_run.py` | Complete executable use | yes | Python 3 source | Python, NumPy, ONNX | `@@REFERENCE_RUN_SHA256@@` |
| Fortran example | `examples/run.f90` | Alternative executable use | no | Fortran source | Fortran compiler and compatible ONNX binding | `@@FORTRAN_SHA256@@` |
| License | `LICENSE` | Legal terms | yes | UTF-8 plain text | Text reader | `@@LICENSE_SHA256@@` |
| Citation | `CITATION.txt` | Citation | yes | UTF-8 plain text | Text reader | `@@CITATION_SHA256@@` |

There are no external artifacts, alternative serializations, custom operators,
or ONNX external-data files.

Detached files `package-description.sha256`, `conformance-report.json`, and
`conformance-report.sha256` are produced after the indexed digest closure. They
are not required for inference.

## Quick start

1. Obtain `package-description.sha256` from a trusted channel and verify the
   exact bytes of `package-description.json`.
2. Read `package-description.json` using the declared direct JSON mapping and
   verify every indexed byte size and SHA-256 digest.
3. Read the physical known-answer values from `verification/nominal.csv`.
4. Bind the two input arrays to the exact ONNX names `temperature_anomaly` and
   `radiative_forcing`.
5. Load `models/linear-response.onnx` and run one inference invocation.
6. Interpret `temperature_response` directly in kelvin; no external transform
   is applied.
7. Compare each result with the expected CSV column using
   `1e-6 K + 1e-6 * abs(expected)`.

The complete reference command is:

```sh
python3 examples/reference_run.py .
```

`examples/run.f90` performs the same known-answer check using a Fortran ONNX
binding. The binding is an example consumer and is not required by this
package's scientific contract.

## Inputs and outputs

### ONNX interface

| ONNX name | Direction | Role | Element type | ONNX logical shape | Axes | External transform |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `temperature_anomaly` | input | physical predictor | `tensor(float)` | `[batch, 1]` | batch, scalar | none |
| `radiative_forcing` | input | physical forcing | `tensor(float)` | `[batch, 1]` | batch, scalar | none |
| `temperature_response` | output | physical response | `tensor(float)` | `[batch, 1]` | batch, scalar | none |

### Scientific quantities

| Tensor | Quantity | Physical units | Graph units | Convention |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `temperature_anomaly` | Temperature anomaly relative to an unspecified reference | K | K | Positive is warmer |
| `radiative_forcing` | Illustrative radiative forcing | W m^-2 | W m^-2 | Positive increases response |
| `temperature_response` | Illustrative temperature response | K | K | Positive is warming |

The unspecified anomaly reference state is acceptable only because no physical
prediction is claimed. A scientific model would have to define it exactly.

### Domain coverage

| Tensor | Admissible domain | Training support | Evaluated support | Intended-use support |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `temperature_anomaly` | finite float32, -100 K to 100 K | no training | exact CSV cases | -10 K to 10 K |
| `radiative_forcing` | finite float32, -2000 W m^-2 to 2000 W m^-2 | no training | exact CSV cases | -500 W m^-2 to 500 W m^-2 |
| `temperature_response` | finite float32 output | no training | exact CSV cases | package testing only |

`batch` is dynamic and positive. The admissible interface is broader than the
tested and intended-use domains. Only batch sizes 1 and 3 are verified.

Missing values, masks, padding, NaNs, and infinities are unsupported. Inputs
containing them must be rejected before inference. Values outside the intended
domain are not supported even if the ONNX runtime accepts them.

Shapes above are in ONNX logical order. In the packaged Fortran example,
`[batch, 1]` is stored as `(1, batch)`.

## Transformations and effective process

External preprocessing consists only of `validate-inputs`, which rejects
non-finite values and values outside `interface-domain`. There is no
normalization, unit conversion, feature construction, or external
postprocessing. The ONNX graph contains `linear-response-equation`:

```text
temperature_response = 0.8 * temperature_anomaly
                     + 0.01 * radiative_forcing
```

where the forcing coefficient has units K m^2 W^-1. ONNX float32 rounding is
part of the graph's numerical behavior.

The effective process graph is:

```text
physical temperature anomaly --+
                               +-> validate -> ONNX linear-response -> physical response
physical radiative forcing ----+
```

The validation boundary rejects non-finite or inadmissible values. It is a
consumer responsibility outside ONNX and is not duplicated inside the graph.

## Coordinates, time, and coupling

The three tensors are non-spatial scalar samples. They have no coordinate
reference system, grid, mesh, timestamp, accumulation interval, boundary
condition, persistent state, or coupling contract. Samples in a batch are
independent. Iterative and coupled use is unsupported.

## Scientific validation

| Claim | Domain | Metric | Result | Baseline | Criterion | Conclusion | Evidence |
|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---|---|
| `known-answer-behavior`: The ONNX graph reproduces the declared formula for the three nominal cases | `verification-domain` | maximum absolute error, K | `@@MAX_ERROR@@` | Direct float32 formula | `<= 1e-6 K + 1e-6 * abs(expected)` | passed | `known-answer-evidence`; `verification/reference-report.json` |
| `scientific-validity`: The coefficients provide a scientifically valid physical prediction | none | not defined | not evaluated | none | none | not supported | `scientific-validity-evidence` |

The nominal result uses three independent table rows. The dynamic-shape result
uses batch size 1. No sampling uncertainty interval is applicable to the
deterministic known-answer comparison. The analytical expression is the
reference, with float32 rounding as the only stated reference uncertainty.

Passing this comparison is package verification, not scientific validation.
No source-model accuracy, physical conservation, calibration, out-of-domain,
long-horizon, or operational evaluation has been performed.

## Verification

`nominal` reads all three CSV rows and checks nonzero, mixed-sign values.
`dynamic-batch` reads the first row and checks batch size 1. Both use ONNX
ReferenceEvaluator from ONNX @@ONNX_VERSION@@ under Python @@PYTHON_VERSION@@,
CPU, float32, with no fallback or custom operators.

The generator also swaps the two same-shaped input arrays as a negative
integration control. The documented comparison rejects the perturbed mapping;
the minimum detected error is `@@PERTURBATION_ERROR@@` K.

The absolute tolerance is larger than the observed float32 rounding difference
but small enough to detect skipped coefficients, swapped inputs, or an incorrect
graph. Relative tolerance prevents an unnecessarily strict comparison as output
magnitude grows.

## Limitations and failure modes

- The coefficients are arbitrary demonstration constants.
- There is no training data, physical reference dataset, baseline model,
  measurement uncertainty, or predictive uncertainty.
- Only three nominal rows and two batch sizes are verified.
- Values may be numerically accepted outside the intended-use domain without a
  detectable output flag.
- The graph does not enforce input-domain or finite-value checks.
- No spatial, temporal, iterative, coupled, stochastic, accelerator, or custom
  operator behavior is evaluated.

Failure outside the known-answer checks cannot be inferred reliably from the
output. No out-of-domain detector is provided.

Unknown behavior includes every untested input shape, runtime, provider,
precision, platform, and scientific condition outside the stated cases.

## Data and model provenance

The model is assembled directly by
`examples/scientific_model_package/build_package.py`. It is not trained. The
initializers contain the literal float32 coefficients 0.8 and 0.01. There are no
source simulations, observations, experiments, instruments, dataset splits,
hyperparameter trials, checkpoints, or fitted transformation parameters.

The builder uses ONNX @@ONNX_VERSION@@ and NumPy to construct an opset-18 graph,
sets ONNX IR version 9, runs the ONNX checker, and executes the known-answer
cases with the ONNX reference evaluator. The graph digest is
`sha256:@@MODEL_SHA256@@`.

## Runtime and compatibility

| Configuration | Runtime | Hardware | Precision | Shapes | Fallback | Verification |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `onnx-reference-cpu` | ONNX @@ONNX_VERSION@@ ReferenceEvaluator | CPU | float32 | `[1,1]`, `[3,1]` | none | passed |

ONNX IR version: 9<br>
Opset imports: default domain version 18<br>
Custom operators: none<br>
External ONNX data: none<br>
Determinism: deterministic for the verified reference configuration

No accelerator, performance, latency, throughput, or memory claim is made.

